    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> had been called to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, but a sequence of economic and political events led the crusader army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The plan had been to restore the deposed Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a>, whose son Alexios IV promised financial and military aid. When the payment did not come, the crusaders captured and plundered the city in April 1204. The leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, among them Boniface I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had planned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> as the Western-recognized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the east.</p><p>On 9 May 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-latin-emperor">Baldwin IX of Flanders</a> was elected emperor with Venetian support and crowned as Baldwin I on 16 May in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, in a ceremony that closely followed Eastern Roman practice. In the Partitio terrarum imperii Romaniae of 1 October 1204, three eighths of the empire, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, went to Venice. The empire claimed the rest, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>: the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and the Duchy of the Archipelago. The name "Latin Empire" was given by historians in the 16th century; its own documents called it the imperium Constantinopolitanum, and the Byzantines spoke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankokratia">Frankokratia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> did not trust the professional Greek bureaucracy and dismantled the Greek economic administration of the lands they took. The result was disastrous, disrupting production and trade, and almost from its start the empire was asking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> for aid. For a few years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> exported wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> still profited from its place on major trade routes. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a> in 1216 there was a major deficit in leadership, and by the 1230s the city, its population drastically reduced, faced a shortage of basic foodstuffs.</p><p>In several senses the only significant export was the sale to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> of relics looted from Greek churches. The elite were Frankish and Venetian lords, headed by the emperor, the barons and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, among them many former Byzantine aristocrats. The bulk of the people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, still divided by the Byzantine system into income classes based on land. Known artwork made within the empire is almost non-existent, but crusader rule made Byzantine art better known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and contributed to the Italo-Byzantine style.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> the crusaders took most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> by 1205, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a> at Poemanenum and Prusa. A truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> followed in 1207, a further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> victory at the Rhyndakos river in October 1211, and the Treaty of Nymphaeum in 1214. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the empire met a powerful enemy at once. At the Battle of Adrianople on 14 April 1205 the Bulgarian tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> and his Cuman allies crushed the Latin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> and captured Emperor Baldwin, who died in prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a> recovered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and campaigned in Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. The Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a> captured and executed Peter of Courtenay, took Thessalonica in 1224 and conquered Thrace in 1225–26, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> itself. In 1224 the Latins were defeated at the battle of Poimanenon and lost nearly all of Asia. In 1235 Nicaea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> besieged Constantinople without success. After the Nicaean victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259, only the Theodosian walls stood between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> and the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire was formed and run on Western European feudal principles, with some elements of the Byzantine bureaucracy. The emperor was assisted by a council of the barons, the Venetian Podestà of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and his six-member council. This council had a major voice in government, above all during regencies, when the Regent (moderator imperii) depended on its consent. The podestà was practically independent of the emperor, ruling the Venetian quarters of Constantinople and Pera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>; he was styled Governor of One-Fourth and One-Half of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and wore the imperial crimson buskins.</p><p>Baldwin's title closely copied that of the Byzantine emperors, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s letters used three versions, Emperor of Romania, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and of Constantinople. After Henry died in 1216 came Peter of Courtenay, his widow Yolanda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, the regents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a> de Béthune and Cardinal Giovanni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a>, Robert of Courtenay, the regent John of Brienne and Baldwin II. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Catholic hierarchy was set up under the Latin patriarch of Constantinople; Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> who accepted papal supremacy kept their sees, and Greek priests served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> majority in a subordinate position.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> created five polities at once that claimed to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>: the Latin Empire, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> and the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>. Possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was the key claim that set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperors apart from the Byzantine rivals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, Trebizond and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. Western sources and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> treated the conquest as a takeover of the eastern empire, not its replacement; a German chronicle of the time, the Deeds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, called Baldwin "emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>".</p><p>The empire never gained political or economic dominance over the other Latin powers set up on former Byzantine land, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which drew lasting profit from its share. Western religious orders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and Dominicans, founded houses in the empire, and disputes with the Greek clergy over ordination, jurisdiction and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> continued. Through the relics and icons carried west, crusader rule over Constantinople fed the Italo-Byzantine style of painting, as in Berlinghiero's Virgin and Child of c. 1230, modelled on the Hodegetria icon type.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Robert of Courtenay died in 1228, a regency under John of Brienne ruled. The Epirote threat ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>'s defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klokotnitsa">Battle of Klokotnitsa</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> took its place. In 1237 Baldwin II came of age and took over a much-diminished state. He travelled often to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> seeking aid, largely without success, and raised money by selling the lead roofs of the Great Palace and pledging his son Philip to Venetian merchants. By 1247 the Nicaeans had effectively surrounded the emperor's main holdings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>An attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> failed in 1260. In March 1261 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> made an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. The general Alexios Strategopoulos, sent with 800 soldiers, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, learned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selymbria">Selymbria</a> that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> garrison and the Venetian fleet were away raiding Daphnousia. On the night of 24/25 July 1261 his men entered through a secret passage and opened a gate. Baldwin II and the Latin inhabitants fled by ship, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> was restored. The imperial title passed to Philip of Courtenay and to the Angevin princes of Taranto, and other Latin states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> lasted, some until the 18th century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader state</a> set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> sacked the city in 1204. Ruled on feudal lines and overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, it lost ground to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> year by year until a small Nicaean force retook the city in 1261 and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</em></p>

